<DetailLayout image="/images/bg-cost.webp" title="Guilin & Yangshuo Trip Cost" subtitle="Real 2026 prices: daily budget breakdown for backpackers, mid-range travelers, and luxury." description="Complete Guilin Yangshuo trip cost guide with real 2026 prices in USD. Budget, mid-range, and luxury daily breakdowns for hotels, food, transport, and activities.">
  <h2>How Much Should You Budget?</h2>
  <p>China is excellent value for money — you get world-class scenery at a fraction of European or North American prices. A 3-day Guilin Yangshuo trip costs roughly what you'd spend on one night in a mid-range Swiss hotel. Here's the full breakdown per person per day:</p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-8">
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-stone-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
      <thead class="bg-stone-50 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-400">
        <tr><th class="px-4 py-3">Item</th><th class="px-4 py-3">Budget</th><th class="px-4 py-3">Mid-Range</th><th class="px-4 py-3">Luxury</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100">
        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Hotel (per night)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$8-15 (hostel/guesthouse)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$30-60 (comfortable hotel)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$100-250 (resort/boutique)</td></tr>
        <tr class="bg-stone-50/50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Meals (3 per day)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$5-10 (street food, noodles)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$15-25 (local restaurants)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$40-80 (fine dining, hotel)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Local transport</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$3-8 (buses, bike rental)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$10-20 (taxis, e-bike rental)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$40-80 (private driver)</td></tr>
        <tr class="bg-stone-50/50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Activities & entry fees</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$5-15 (hiking, free viewpoints)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$20-40 (Li River cruise, Moon Hill)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$60-120 (private tours, all activities)</td></tr>
        <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Daily total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$20-50</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$75-145</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$240-530</td></tr>
        <tr class="font-semibold bg-accent/5"><td class="px-4 py-3">3-day total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$60-150</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$225-435</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$720-1,590</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-400">All prices in USD at April 2026 exchange rates (~7.2 RMB per USD). Actual costs vary by season — expect 20-30% higher during Golden Week and Chinese New Year.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-10">What the Budget Tier Looks Like</h2>
  <p>Stay in a hostel dorm or basic guesthouse (80-120 RMB). Eat at local rice noodle shops and street food stalls (8-15 RMB per meal). Take public buses between Guilin and Yangshuo (25 RMB). Hike to free viewpoints like Laozhai Mountain and the 20 RMB banknote spot. Rent a bicycle (30 RMB/day) instead of taking taxis. It's completely doable and many backpackers travel this way.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-10">What the Mid-Range Tier Looks Like</h2>
  <p>Stay in a comfortable hotel or countryside guesthouse with mountain views (200-400 RMB). Eat at good local restaurants — try beer fish and stuffed snails (60-100 RMB for dinner for two). Take the Li River bamboo raft (120-250 RMB). Use taxis or Didi for longer trips. This is the sweet spot — you get a great experience without watching every yuan.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-10">Hidden Costs People Forget</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>China visa:</strong> $140 (US citizens) — budget this if you're not using TWOV</li>
    <li><strong>VPN subscription:</strong> $5-15/month — set up before you leave</li>
    <li><strong>eSIM / data:</strong> $5-15 for a 7-day travel eSIM</li>
    <li><strong>Travel insurance:</strong> $30-60 for a week — strongly recommended</li>
    <li><strong>Tips:</strong> Not expected or required in China. Don't budget for them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10">Cash or Card?</h2>
  <p>China runs on mobile payments — Alipay and WeChat Pay. Set these up before you travel if possible. Many rural vendors are cash-only, so carry at least 500 RMB in cash. International credit cards are accepted at major hotels and some restaurants, but not at small shops, street stalls, or local transport. ATMs at Bank of China, ICBC, and China Construction Bank accept foreign cards.</p>

